{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "\paragraph{HellaSwag Accuracy.}
Zero-shot normalized accuracy (acc\_norm) on HellaSwag~\cite{zellers2019hellaswag} via \texttt{lm-evaluation-harness}~\cite{gao2024lmeval}, measuring downstream commonsense reasoning capability.
HellaSwag ($\uparrow$) & \textbf{0.4130} & 0.3897 & 0.3764 & 0.3658 & 0.3558 & 0.3439 & 0.3311 & 0.3187 \\
HellaSwag accuracy provides downstream task evidence for representational collapse.  Accuracy drops continuously from 0.4130 (pretrained) through 0.3897 (Gen~1) to 0.3439 (Gen~5), a total decline of 16.7\% from the pretrained checkpoint and 11.7\% from the Gen~1 baseline.  This confirms that spectral collapse is not merely an embedding-space phenomenon but propagates to downstream commonsense reasoning capability.  The HellaSwag trajectory parallels the spectral indicators with no lag, declining monotonically at every generation.
A critical temporal asymmetry emerges from comparing the spectral and functional indicators.  Functional metrics (perplexity, HellaSwag) decline from Generation~1 onward, while spectral indicators peak at Generation~2 and reverse at Generation~3, revealing a two-generation detection delay when using spectral inflection as the warning signal.  This lag does not diminish the utility of spectral early-warning signals; rather, it highlights their complementary role.  Spectral metrics offer two practical advantages over functional indicators: (1)~they require no text generation, labeled benchmarks, or external evaluation sets, only a single forward pass through a small unlabeled probe set ($\sim$1K sequences), and (2)~they provide a geometric interpretation of the collapse mechanism (loss of representational volume) rather than merely measuring downstream symptoms.  These properties make spectral signals suitable for closed-loop control, where evaluation cost and latency are critical constraints.
